{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Node1 provides an operational POST /upload endpoint on TCP port 8080 that is reachable from node2 and survives restart.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 listened on 0.0.0.0:8080 and accepted uploads from node2. After node1 rebooted to a different boot identity, the service, firewall rule, existing data, and a fresh hash-matched upload recovered successfully.","evidence_ids":["cmd-656ee65db7074002","cmd-00b10495c0df4c60","cmd-30cdf9ddf7f148e0","cmd-f322fa0f81d24190","cmd-52dfda4e9df74887","cmd-fdd90d6b4bef4f3f","cmd-78f9d70b58b0429f","cmd-d04c9cbd9cd74c91"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Accept raw request bodies through 65,536 bytes, storing every accepted upload in a separate file with identical bytes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A 257-byte body and repeated 65,536-byte bodies returned HTTP 200. Server-side sizes and MD5 hashes matched the client payloads, and the two boundary uploads appeared as distinct receipt files.","evidence_ids":["cmd-00b10495c0df4c60","cmd-9548d2ea1bc84b22","cmd-8d239a1fdf034c81","cmd-f5fe3e8e0f914698"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Reject request bodies larger than 65,536 bytes with HTTP 413 before publishing a receipt.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both pre-restart and final 65,537-byte requests returned HTTP 413. Final inspection found no receipt with the oversized payload's checksum, while boundary-sized receipts existed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-00b10495c0df4c60","cmd-9548d2ea1bc84b22","cmd-8d239a1fdf034c81","cmd-f5fe3e8e0f914698"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"An interrupted upload leaves no completed receipt.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Two requests were deliberately closed after sending fewer bytes than declared. Receipt counts did not increase, no corresponding partial-sized receipt existed, and the private staging directory was empty afterward.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fac61e6b5d744ef2","cmd-87c11ff2c5eb4caa","cmd-8d239a1fdf034c81","cmd-f5fe3e8e0f914698"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Paths other than /upload return HTTP 404.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct GET and POST requests to non-upload paths returned HTTP 404 with empty bodies, including checks after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-00b10495c0df4c60","cmd-30cdf9ddf7f148e0","cmd-fdd90d6b4bef4f3f","cmd-faeb08428c6340e4","cmd-8d239a1fdf034c81"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Preserve the existing receipt in /srv/legacy2/http-upload-size-boundary.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The seeded prior.bin was 28 bytes before repair and remained 28 bytes with the same MD5 after implementation, testing, and restart. Its final contents also remained alongside the new receipts.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-a4b22afe0bfa4ac2","cmd-30cdf9ddf7f148e0","cmd-fdd90d6b4bef4f3f","cmd-f5fe3e8e0f914698"]},{"id":"R7","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 according to fixed before/after platform observations. Node1 remained CentOS 5.11, the handler compiled under Python 2.4, and it operated successfully after reboot.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","cmd-654be0c2da614ceb","cmd-faeb08428c6340e4","cmd-f5fe3e8e0f914698"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.92,"summary":"System mutations were causally connected to implementing the endpoint, enabling node2 access, validating behavior, or performing the mandatory restart protocol. The initially invalid server rewrite in cmd-525ab8174dbb4d1f was corrected into the final implementation rather than being an unrelated experiment. Minor avoidable residue remained on node2: five upload payload fixtures created by cmd-00b10495c0df4c60, cmd-78f9d70b58b0429f, and cmd-faeb08428c6340e4 were absent after preparation but present after execution. A pre-repair server backup was also created by cmd-525ab8174dbb4d1f and no cleanup was recorded, although its final presence was not directly inventoried. Contract-compliant receipt files created by the behavior tests were necessary evidence and were not treated as unrelated mutations. No package changes, failed services, or weakened security controls were demonstrated.","evidence_ids":["cmd-525ab8174dbb4d1f","cmd-654be0c2da614ceb","cmd-656ee65db7074002","cmd-b5a7a228a64340f7","cmd-00b10495c0df4c60","cmd-78f9d70b58b0429f","cmd-faeb08428c6340e4","global:after_prepare:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied by direct behavior and final-state evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also strong for both managed nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart observation, restart acknowledgement, observed downtime, a changed boot identity corroborated by fixed lifecycle snapshots, and recovered client/service/data behavior. Hygiene was good overall, with only small retained test fixtures and a possibly retained backup reducing the score.","limitations":["The tests directly sampled 257-byte, 65,536-byte, and 65,537-byte payloads rather than exhaustively exercising every possible size; the captured handler and boundary results consistently support the requested behavior.","The fixed lifecycle listening-socket observations were empty even though contemporaneous direct netstat evidence showed port 8080 listening; the direct task-specific evidence was therefore given greater weight.","Systemd failed-service collection was unsupported on this legacy OS, and the lifecycle package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum operation, limiting system-wide hygiene visibility.","No final top-level inventory of node1's application directory was captured, so continued presence of server.py.pre-repair is inferred from its creation and the absence of a recorded cleanup rather than directly observed."]}
